<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Muthul</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">109 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurthine-war">Jugurthine War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Muthul was fought in 109 BCE near the Muthul River in Numidia between the Roman army of the consul Quintus Caecilius Metellus and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>. Jugurtha ambushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> on the march, but after a hard day's fighting Metellus drove off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a>. The battle did not end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurthine-war">Jugurthine War</a>, as Jugurtha turned to raids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Metellus, assisted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> and Publius Rutilius Rufus, led his army into the part of Numidia that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> had received at the partition of the kingdom. The Muthul has been identified with the Wäd Mellag in south-east Numidia, though Mannert and Forbiger identified it with the river Ubus in the west. Between the river and a barren range of mountains about twenty miles away lay a dry plain, crossed by a hill covered with wild olive and myrtle.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> drew up his army in a long line on that hill, at a right angle to the Roman road. He gave Bomilcar the elephants and part of the infantry, and kept the cavalry and the best infantry with himself nearer the mountain, reminding his men that they had sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under the yoke at the Battle of Suthul.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Descending the mountain, Metellus spotted the hidden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a>, halted, and re-formed his army with a triple reserve and cavalry on the flanks. Fearing thirst, he sent Rutilius ahead with light <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> and cavalry to secure a camp by the river, while he advanced slowly with Marius commanding the rear. Once the Roman rear had passed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> sent about 2,000 men to seize the hill behind them and attacked from all sides. His cavalry scattered when pursued and closed in again, and for a quarter of the day the fighting was confused and indecisive.</p><p>As the Numidian attacks slackened in the heat, Metellus rallied his men and sent four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> against the infantry on the high ground, and towards evening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> stormed the hill, though its defenders escaped. By the river, Bomilcar attacked Rutilius's camp, but his men gave way once the elephants were caught in the trees and surrounded. In the darkness the two Roman forces nearly fought each other until scouts discovered the truth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> disbanded most of his troops and turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a>, the news was greeted with joy and public thanksgivings were decreed. Metellus was later checked at Zama, and Marius, elected consul against the Senate's wishes, enlisted volunteers from the capite censi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> without property, and went on to capture Jugurtha in 106 BCE. The Senate nonetheless gave Metellus the title Numidicus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Muthul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Numidian warfare: Jugurtha bribed Roman commanders, forced an army under Aulus Postumius Albinus to pass under the yoke near Suthul in 110 BCE, fought Quintus Caecilius Metellus at the <b>Battle of the Muthul</b> in 109 BCE and then turned to raids and mobile war in the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
